libimagequant: make quantisation independent of the architecture

The same image quantises to a different palette on x86_64 and aarch64
(for noto-emoji's UN flag: 111 colours on x86_64, 120 on aarch64; 92 of
the 4,004 Noto Color Emoji bitmaps differed between the architectures'
builds). Two things in libimagequant depend on the architecture:

1. Histogram order. The colour histogram lives in a HashMap and is read
   back in iteration order in two places: Histogram::finalize_builder turns
   the map into the item list (`temp.extend(self.hashmap.values()...)`),
   and init_posterize_bits re-keys the map when it exceeds
   max_histogram_entries, where collapsing keys are resolved last-wins.
   Everything downstream of the item list is order-sensitive (cluster
   bucketing by position, an f64 weight sum, median cut tie-breaking,
   k-means 256-item chunks). The hasher is deterministic, but hashbrown's
   probe-group width is 16 bytes with SSE2 on x86_64 and 8 bytes with NEON
   on aarch64, so the map iterates in a different order on each. Sort the
   entries by colour before the cluster pass and re-insert the posterized
   entries in key order.

2. Colour-distance addition order. f_pixel::diff has hand-written SIMD
   paths; the x86_64 and scalar paths sum the three channel terms as
   (r + g) + b, the NEON path used a pairwise add that sums r + (g + b).
   Float addition is not associative, so the distance metric itself differed
   by an ulp here and there, which the refinement loop turns into a
   different palette for some images (visible with --quality 0-100). Make
   the NEON path add in the same order as the others.

With both changes the x86_64 and aarch64 builds produce identical pixels
and palettes for every image tested. Output on each architecture also
changes for images where either effect mattered: same quality target, same
algorithm, a fixed tie order and a consistent metric.

Upstream: https://github.com/ImageOptim/libimagequant/issues/130, fix proposed in
https://github.com/ImageOptim/libimagequant/pull/131 (same three changes against main).
Applies to the libimagequant submodule pngquant 3.0.3 pins (6e980576).

diff --git a/lib/src/hist.rs b/lib/src/hist.rs
index 99662ed..fb9922d 100644
--- a/lib/src/hist.rs
+++ b/lib/src/hist.rs
@@ -248,7 +248,13 @@ impl Histogram {
 
         let new_size = (self.hashmap.len()/3).max(self.hashmap.capacity()/5);
         let old_hashmap = std::mem::replace(&mut self.hashmap, HashMap::with_capacity_and_hasher(new_size, U32Hasher(0)));
-        self.hashmap.extend(old_hashmap.into_iter().map(move |(k, v)| {
+        // Re-insert in key order: when several keys collapse to one posterized
+        // key the last one wins, and the old map's iteration order depends on
+        // the hash table's bucket layout (hashbrown's group width differs
+        // between x86_64 and aarch64). See libimagequant#130.
+        let mut entries: Vec<(u32, (u32, RGBA))> = old_hashmap.into_iter().collect();
+        entries.sort_unstable_by_key(|&(k, _)| k);
+        self.hashmap.extend(entries.into_iter().map(move |(k, v)| {
             (k & new_posterize_mask, v)
         }));
     }
@@ -304,6 +310,10 @@ impl Histogram {
             let weight = boost as f32;
             TempHistItem { color, weight, cluster_index }
         }));
+        // Make the histogram order independent of the hash table's bucket layout
+        // (hashbrown's group width differs between x86_64 and aarch64), so the
+        // result does not depend on the architecture. See libimagequant#130.
+        temp.sort_unstable_by_key(|t| (t.color.r, t.color.g, t.color.b, t.color.a, t.cluster_index));
 
         let mut clusters = [Cluster { begin: 0, end: 0 }; LIQ_MAXCLUSTER];
         let mut next_begin = 0;
diff --git a/lib/src/pal.rs b/lib/src/pal.rs
index da42d73..58a8a53 100644
--- a/lib/src/pal.rs
+++ b/lib/src/pal.rs
@@ -74,12 +74,12 @@ impl f_pixel {
             let mut max_r = [0.; 4];
             vst1q_f32(max_r.as_mut_ptr(), max);
 
-            let mut max_gb = [0.; 4];
-            vst1q_f32(max_gb.as_mut_ptr(), vpaddq_f32(max, max));
-
-            // add rgb, not a
-            
-            max_r[1] + max_gb[1]
+            // add rgb, not a. Add in the same order as the x86_64 and scalar
+            // paths, (r + g) + b: the pairwise add used here before summed
+            // r + (g + b), and float addition is not associative, so the
+            // distance metric, and with it the palette, differed between
+            // architectures. See libimagequant#130.
+            max_r[1] + max_r[2] + max_r[3]
         }
     }
 
